{
  "dom_id": "step-q223674",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "The axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=-(5x+2)(x-6)$ is $x=${{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "From the equation $y=-(5x+2)(x-6),$ we find that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{5}$ and ${x}_{2}=6.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{2}{5}+6)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{30}{5})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{28}{5})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the axis of symmetry is $x=\\frac{14}{5}.$",
